[
  {
    "number": 1261,
    "title": "hk v2 breaking-change rollout",
    "url": "https://github.com/jdx/hk/discussions/1261",
    "author": "jdx",
    "createdAt": "2026-08-26T06:44:06Z",
    "body": "hk v2 will include a coordinated set of breaking changes. I’d really appreciate feedback on the direction, especially on anything that feels surprising, unclear, or unnecessarily difficult to migrate.

I don't do big breaking changes like this in CLIs very often at all, but early design decisions have come home to roost, and I think one big break—this likely would be the only big change to the design ever—is necessary. The goal is to address those design issues together so hk has a cleaner, more consistent foundation going forward, rather than asking users to work through a series of smaller breaking changes over time.

## Major changes at a glance

### Define shared steps once

In v1, the same steps often had to be repeated across hooks:

```pkl
hooks {
  ["check"] {
    steps {
      ["prettier"] = Builtins.prettier
    }
  }
  ["fix"] {
    fix = true
    steps {
      ["prettier"] = Builtins.prettier
    }
  }
  ["pre-commit"] {
    fix = true
    steps {
      ["prettier"] = Builtins.prettier
    }
  }
}
```

In v2, top-level steps automatically create the usual `check`, `fix`, and `pre-commit` behavior:

```pkl
steps {
  ["prettier"] = Builtins.prettier
}
```

You can still define explicit hooks when you need to override those defaults, and `enabled = false` disables an implicit hook.

### Configure builtins through stable factories

Default builtin references stay the same:

```pkl
["prettier"] = Builtins.prettier
```

Special-purpose builtin names become options on the main builtin. For example:

```pkl
// v1
["gitleaks"] = Builtins.gitleaks_staged
["knip"] = Builtins.knip_strict
["pinact"] = Builtins.pinact_v3

// v2
["gitleaks"] = (Builtins.gitleaks) {
  staged = true
}
["knip"] = (Builtins.knip) {
  strict = true
}
["pinact"] = (Builtins.pinact) {
  version = "3"
}
```

Generic step customization moves under `step`, leaving room for builtin-specific options:

```pkl
// v1
["prettier"] = (Builtins.prettier) {
  batch = false
}

// v2
["prettier"] = (Builtins.prettier) {
  step {
    batch = false
  }
}
```

### Stage fixes only when intended

In v2, `pre-commit` continues to fix and stage changes by default. Manual `hk fix` and other hooks leave changes unstaged unless requested:

```sh
hk fix          # fixes files but leaves them unstaged
hk fix --stage  # fixes and stages them
```

A step-level `stage` pattern now filters which paths are staged; it no longer turns staging on by itself.

### Use the canonical config and command names

| v1 | v2 |
| --- | --- |
| `hk.toml`, `hk.yaml`, `hk.yml`, or `hk.json` | `hk.pkl` |
| project `.hkrc.pkl` | `hk.local.pkl` |
| `~/.hkrc.pkl` | `~/.config/hk/config.pkl` |
| `--hkrc <PATH>` | the XDG or project-local config path |
| `UserConfig.pkl` | `Config.pkl` |
| `environment { ... }` | `env { ... }` |
| `hk generate` | `hk init` |
| `HK_PKL_BACKEND` | remove it; hk always uses its built-in pklr evaluator |

Removed v1 entry points will produce targeted migration errors rather than failing without context.

## Implementation

The implementation is split across a few stacked PRs, which are intended to merge in this order:

1. [#1253 — `feat(builtins)!: expose configurable builtin factories`](https://github.com/jdx/hk/pull/1253)
2. [#1255 — `feat(config)!: add shared top-level steps`](https://github.com/jdx/hk/pull/1255)
3. [#1256 — `feat(hook)!: make staging contextual`](https://github.com/jdx/hk/pull/1256)
4. [#1257 — `refactor(config)!: remove deprecated v1 interfaces`](https://github.com/jdx/hk/pull/1257)
5. [#1270 — `refactor(config)!: remove pkl CLI fallback`](https://github.com/jdx/hk/pull/1270)

`hk install --legacy` will remain supported because it controls Git-hook installation compatibility, not v1 configuration compatibility.

If you use hk today, please share your thoughts below. Feedback on the proposed syntax, migration pain points, error messages, or anything else that would make this transition friendlier would be especially helpful.

## Release sequencing

For maintainers: all breaking PRs should merge in order before the release-plz v2 release PR. Feature branches will not set `2.0.0`; release-plz will derive the major release from the Conventional Commit `!` titles.

*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: unavailable/unavailable; version: unavailable.*",
    "comments": [
      {
        "author": "risu729",
        "body": "I maintain a shared Pkl preset ([risu729/hk-config](https://github.com/risu729/hk-config)) that wraps the builtins catalog for my repos, and migrated it against the branch schemas to see how the changes hold up in practice: [risu729/hk-config#83](https://github.com/risu729/hk-config/pull/83). Overall the direction is great — the implicit `check`/`fix`/`pre-commit` hooks matched the hook boilerplate my preset generated (including `stash = git` on pre-commit) so exactly that I could delete the helper that existed only to produce it. Findings, each checked against the branch code:

### Bug: top-level `steps` rejects builtin factories

Introduced in #1255: top-level `steps` is typed `Mapping<String, Step | Group>` while `Hook.steps` and `Group.steps` in the same file use `StepDefinition`. So the headline example fails typecheck:

```pkl
steps {
  ["prettier"] = Builtins.prettier // Expected value of type `Step | Group`, but got type `Builtins#Prettier`
}
```

Reproduced by evaluating a config against `pkl/Config.pkl` from `refactor/remove-v1-interfaces` plus a `Builtins.pkl` stub in the generated factory shape. `steps: Mapping<String, StepDefinition | Group>` fixes it.

### Overlook: migration hints don't cover the renamed builtin factories

The hint mechanism in `config.rs` (scanning the config source and Pkl stderr on eval failure) gives targeted guidance for `check_byte_order_marker`/`fix_byte_order_marker`, `UserConfig.pkl`, `Types.Regex`, etc. — but not for `gitleaks_staged`, `knip_strict`, `pinact_v3`, or `pinact_update_v3`, the four renames this discussion announces. Those currently surface as a bare Pkl "Cannot find property" error. Adding them to the hint list (with the factory-option replacement, e.g. `(Builtins.gitleaks) { staged = true }`) would make `test/v2_migration_errors.bats` coverage match the announcement.

### Small builtin fix while breaking anyway: pinact glob misses root `action.yml`

`pinact`'s `glob = List(".github/workflows/*", "*/action.*")`: since step globs are built without `literal_separator`, `*` crosses directories and nested metadata like `.github/actions/setup/action.yml` already matches — but a root-level `action.yml` (standalone action repos) never does, because the pattern requires a `/` before `action.`. `**/action.*` covers both.

### Suggestion: a convention for strictness options

`knip.strict` and `pinact.version` establish factory options nicely. It would help preset authors if common strictness toggles became factory options too (e.g. zizmor `--pedantic`, hadolint `--failure-threshold`): today the only way to get them is replacing the command under `step { … }`, which also downgrades the v2 structured `CommandSpec` (argv + effect metadata) back to an opaque shell string. A loose convention — `strict: Boolean` wherever the tool has a pedantic/strict mode — would keep those overrides declarative and preserve the effect metadata.

### Docs suggestion

The factory-option + `step { … }` interaction is the main thing preset authors will reach for, e.g.:

```pkl
["pinact"] = (Builtins.pinact) {
  version = "3"
  step { glob = List(".github/workflows/*", "**/action.*") }
}
```

I verified Pkl's late binding makes the `step` amend apply on top of the version-selected step, but that's non-obvious enough to deserve an explicit example in the migration doc.

*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-fable-5; version: 2.1.241.*",
        "url": "https://github.com/jdx/hk/discussions/1261#discussioncomment-18162115",
        "createdAt": "2026-08-26T12:06:23Z"
      },
      {
        "author": "EgoMaw",
        "body": "I think top level steps can introduce some confusion if its not clearly conveyed what hooks they create.

Builtins having options instead of duplicating them for specific flows is a welcomed change.
And to expand further, would that open the road for passing our own arguments over whatever the builtin currently passes, in the future?",
        "url": "https://github.com/jdx/hk/discussions/1261#discussioncomment-18162746",
        "createdAt": "2026-08-26T13:03:15Z"
      },
      {
        "author": "bchatard",
        "body": "I'm not sure if this is the right place to bring this up, but v2 seems like a good candidate.

Since pklr is a solid replacement for the default Pkl processing when using hk, would it be possible to support the same built-ins? https://hk.jdx.dev/builtins.html#pkl",
        "url": "https://github.com/jdx/hk/discussions/1261#discussioncomment-18184182",
        "createdAt": "2026-08-28T05:41:37Z"
      }
    ]
  }
]

